Denote by $\chiup_{
u,\shskip l} $ the character of $\BCx$ given by  egin{align*} \chiup_{
u,\shskip l} : z a \|z\|^{ 
u} (z/|z|)^{l}, \end{align*} with $
u \in \BC$ and $ l \in \BZ $.

According to Langlands' classification for $G$, any irreducible admissible representation of $G $ may be parametrized by $(
u_1, 
u_2, l_1, l_2) \in \BC^2 	imes \BZ^2$. First, we introduce the principal series representation $\pi (\chiup_{
u_1, \shskip l_1}, \chiup_{
u_2,\shskip l_2})$. It is known that $\mathrm{Ind}_B^G (\chiup_{
u_1, \shskip l_1}, \chiup_{
u_2,\shskip l_2}) $, defined by the unitary induction, has a unique irreducible quotient provided that $\Re 
u_1 \geqslant \Re 
u_2$. We denote this quotient by $\pi (\chiup_{
u_1, \shskip l_1}, \chiup_{
u_2,\shskip l_2})$ according to \cite[\S 6]{J-L}. Second, these principal series $\pi (\chiup_{
u_1, \shskip l_1}, \chiup_{
u_2,\shskip l_2})$ exhaust all the irreducible admissible representations of $G$ up to infinitesimal equivalence that occurs when we permute $ \chiup_{
u_1, \shskip l_1}$ and $ \chiup_{
u_2,\shskip l_2}$. Note that we can always permute $( 
u_1, l_1 )$ and $(
u_2, l_2)$ if necessary so that $\Re 
u_1 \geqslant \Re 
u_2$ is satisfied. See \cite[\S 4]{Knapp} and \cite[\S 6]{J-L} for more details.
